A||||17||FIEROCIOUS||Spring Rates Again||||5||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||03-05-2000||08:24 PM||||What spring rates should I consider for the rear suspension with coil overs?<BR>I have a 87 GT 5spd/6cyl with KYBs.<BR>I know this has been asked before but no one ever replys! So PLEASE reply!<BR>P.S. I have Eibach springs in front what are the spring rates on those also?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||03-05-2000||08:51 PM|||| Ryanes Motor Sports suggests 250-300 for sport/street  325-375 Hi Perf Street  400-500 slalom/autocross.comp||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||03-05-2000||09:53 PM||||I have the JC Whitney Catalog here in front of me with the order form filled out. Does any one have the sport springs they sell? If so what is the ride like? and it doesn't give a spring rate??? Also have Ground Controls rear coil over adjustable spring kit in my sites. I'm ready to drop The Little Black Toy. What should I do?  <P>"So many to buy and so little time"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-05-2000||09:56 PM||||I'm going with a 350 pound spring rate to start out with on the rear of my V8 Fiero. I was thinking about 300# springs, but was told by Darrel at RCC that with more weight and more power output of a V8, I'd need a heavier spring. Ask LowCG here on the Forum, he'll know what springs work. I'll bet a 300 pound rate will work pretty good for you if you want a stiffer ride.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||03-05-2000||10:16 PM||||  Sorry to ask such an ignorrant question, but what exactly is "spring rate"? I am a novice so please pardon my naivete'.<P>Thanx BAM-BAM||||reg||3
Z||000005||batboy||03-05-2000||11:07 PM||||A coil spring with a spring rate of 300 pounds will compress one inch if you put a 300 lbs. weight onto the top of it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BAM-BAM||03-05-2000||11:56 PM||||Thanks BatBoy, Makes perfect sense. ||||reg||6
Z||000007||batboy||03-06-2000||12:10 PM||||Dad, I have those front sport springs from JC Whitney, they're the Suspension Techniques springs that will give you about 1-1/2 inch drop. I had to go to smaller tires, so my drop was closer to 2 inches when it was all done. I haven't figured out the spring rate yet, and I've even been to the S/T website. I was going to email them, but never did.<P>I have yet to get an alignment done since I've done my front end work, but it definitely stiffens up the suspension. Of course I did the poly bushings at the same time too, so part of the stiffer ride is from those. I haven't driven it enough yet to give you a good critique. As soon as I get this rear suspension work done and the four wheel alignment, then it's fun time!||||reg||1
Z||000008||86_Fastback||03-06-2000||06:55 PM||||I am getting coil-overs for the rear of my 86 GT from Shelby Custom Exotics.  Shelby suggested a 350 spring to go with the Eibachs I'm putting in the front.||||reg||1
Z||000009||gixxer||03-06-2000||07:27 PM||||TO BATBOY<BR>FYI: to find the lbs.per inch of a coil spring (non-progressive).<BR>LBS per inch= 1,500,000 X d*4<BR>              ---------------<BR>                 n X D*3<P>should read: 1.5 million  times the wire diameter (d) to the forth power,<BR>divided by<BR>the number of active coils (n) times the coil diameter (D) to the third power.<BR>Of course, the coil diameter is measured from the center of the spring wire.<BR>Hope this comes out readable.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DXR_DAD||03-06-2000||08:16 PM||||Batboy thanks, I'm going to order the fronts and get Ground Controls Adjustable Spring kit for the rear. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> And.....hum.....gixxer I now know all I need to know about springs?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I Think?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000011||FIEROCIOUS||03-07-2000||03:04 AM||||Hey, I love all of you! Isn't this forum great? Special thanks to Phil,86_Fastback, and batboy! <BR>P.S. Formula for spring rate is:<BR>G= Torsional modules for steel=11.25x10 to the sixth power or 11,250,000.<BR>d= Wire diameter in inches.<BR>N= Number of ACTIVE coils.<BR>D= Mean coil diameter in inches.<BR>8= A constant for all coil springs.<P>Gd4<BR>----<BR>8ND3<BR>OR:<BR>11,250,000 x .62 x .62 x .62 x .62<BR>---------------------------------<BR>8 x 10 x 5.0 x 5.0 x 5.0<P>1,662,337<BR>-------  = 166 lbs-in<BR>10,000<P>This assumes a wire diameter of .62 in.<BR>a coil diameter of 5.0 in. and<BR>10 ACTIVE coils.<P>gixxer, I think you are useing the torsional stiffness of some other alloy. This formula is for spring or tool steel.<P>I think I will go with 350 lb-in springs, has anyone used this spring rate? How is the ride?<BR>I am thinking of shelby customs set.I've looked at them all, One comment about the ground controls, if you look at their opening page the Civics spring is not sitting in the spring perch! That is why I am buying the coil overs in the first place! My Eibachs move around when I jack it up! This may be acceptable for some but not me! <P>Thanks again and if you have more info please post it!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000012||batboy||03-07-2000||10:21 AM||||The 350# springs will noticeably stiffen up the rear end, especially with a V6. I was told that when I installed my new high hp/high torque small block engine, I might have to increase my spring rate up to 400#. Fortunately, it's fairly simple to change springs, not to mention quite a bit of height adjustment with the coil-overs. I'm going to try the 350# springs for now. Sort of wish I'd converted the front to coil-overs too.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||03-07-2000||06:29 PM||||Batboy,I just installed a set of progressive rate springs in the front of my car,haven't tried them yet as the battery is dead after sitting most of the winter.The calculated rate is from 190 pounds per inch,to 305 pounds per inch,however,I haven't measured the rate at the height(s) that they're installed in the car to be sure of the accuracy of these numbers.I'm just guessing that they're at the 230-260 zone in their travel.I have to remove them to take an eighth of an inch off the shims to lower the car a little,maybe I can somehow find a way to measure them when I do this.<BR>One thing that will really make a difference with any stiffer spring is a shock that'll keep up with the higher frequency bounce that's produced as a result,as well as having good rebound control(Koni,Bilstein are good).||||reg||1
Z||000014||gixxer||03-07-2000||07:22 PM||||Fierocious....Well, this is interesting!<BR>Running your numbers thru the formula that I presented netted 177.3.<BR>Hmmm.<BR>The formula is verbatum from a book called Race Car Engineering and Mechanics by Van Valkenburg. His math (formulas) are quick and dirty. The path he follows to arrive at his formulas are not shown at all. <BR>I noticed your numbers could have come straight from Millikens' book.<BR>In any event, I think the formula you presented is the BETTER one.<BR>Chow fer now...<BR>Gixxer.<P>||||reg||11
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||03-07-2000||11:46 PM||||gixxer, The formula listed above is from Herb Adams.<BR>Note if the numbers are off even a little bit you'll get the wrong spring rate.<P>The Eibach front springs are 8 coils long 5 coils are active the wire diameter is .5 in. and the coil diameter is 4 in. Spring rate of Eibachs = 274 lb/in. or 20% stiffer.<P>Stock for my car = 228 lb/in. Stock 9 coils long 6 coils active.<P>The rears realy can't be figured because they are progressive, but I'll take a guess and say the stock rears are 275 lb/in. and the Eibach rears are 330 lb/in. or 20% stiffer. This is at mid travel.<P>Funny, I flunked math in high school!<BR>Notice that I came up with the same number by the calculation as adding 20% Wierd!<P><BR>Mike  ||||reg||6
Z||000016||lowCG||03-08-2000||09:20 PM||||One thing I overlooked when measuring the stock front coils is that the diameter is 4.25,not 4 inches.<BR>All springs are progressive to a certain degree,that's how they work to be springs,so,to measure at one point in the travel would be only part of the picture.What happens to the rate,or pressure of the spring as the suspension travels determines the feel/function of the car's suspension.<BR>Since it was 78 degrees here in Dearborn today,I decided to tear the front springs(Moog Cargo Coils for a VW Jetta)out and adjust my shims,and I didn't measure the rate.However,the two coils that privide the softer ride were nearly flattened,leaving about 3/4 of an inch of soft ride before the firmness.This should help to get rid of the front end dive problem,and a small rear swaybar will be just the ticket to balance things out once again.||||reg||1
Z||000017||FIEROCIOUS||03-09-2000||01:06 AM||||lowCG, When you measure the coil diameter it is measured by center diameter, not outer diameter! Alot of people make this mistake!<P>Coils are not progressive unless the coils are wound in progressively tighter spirals or the spaceing between coils are differant from coil to coil.<BR>A linear rate springs will change rate if the spring undergoes coil stacking or coil bind. Adding shimms to increase sprig rate won't work, this is a common misconception. If you add blocks to a spring the rate of the spring is exactly the same, the car just sits higher.<BR>I've raced cars since birth and I've picked up a few things over the years.<P><BR>Mike||||reg||12
